From 34d32bd3b49e64ac28284fb4b3d0ad75b8d11302 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: salvamalfa <salvamalfas@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 03:46:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/19] Add IPF route-carrier estimator with a measured
 validation harness

AFAC publishes passengers per route and passengers per carrier, never the
cell. This fits a supply seed to both published marginals so the split can
be estimated, and measures the result instead of asserting it.

Estimator (src/analytics/route_carrier.py):
- fit_ipf preserves structural zeros, reconciles marginals that disagree
  (the two AFAC files differ by 46 passengers over 2025Q1) and reports the
  scale factor rather than hiding it, raises InfeasibleMarginsError when a
  marginal demands passengers where no carrier flies, and returns
  convergence diagnostics alongside the matrix.
- estimate_route_carrier returns the tidy estimate together with per-period
  diagnostics, flagging single-carrier routes as exact.
- load_afac_domestic_margins wires both published marginals from reference.

Validation harness: backtest_against_t100 hides the carrier split of the
T-100 panel already in gold, rebuilds it from the two derived marginals and
reports passenger-weighted error on competitive routes. Measured over 2025:
1.40 pp / 4.21% with seats as seed, 1.98 pp / 5.96% with flight counts only.

Convergence needed relative tolerance, not absolute: a threshold in whole
passengers sits below float64 resolution on marginals of tens of millions.
The transborder panel also needs ~1,500 sweeps, so the iteration cap is
5,000.

Tests cover exact recovery, reproduction of both marginals, the row- and
column-scaling invariances that let flight counts substitute for seats,
structural zeros, infeasible marginals, marginal reconciliation, that
fitting both marginals beats splitting by supply alone, and an accuracy
envelope that guards the headline numbers against regressions.

Adds monthly domestic carrier marginals for 2025Q1, reconciled to the AFAC
bulletin at 14,799,050, and documents the seed contract that remains the
one missing input.

